WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:06.660
Only a few months ago,

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:10.660
the term NFT was exploding
all over the internet.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:11.540
Now to the latest trend,

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.690
the skyrocketing prices for
digital arts sold as NFTs-

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.570
The Christie's auction for
digital artist, Beeple,

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:20.540
coming to a close a few moments ago,

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.997
the final bid, $69 million.

00:00:23.997 --> 00:00:26.450
$69 million.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:28.680
I think it probably means digital artists

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.320
here to stay.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.300
Anybody in this sector has to admit

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.840
that they were shocked by the size

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.350
and the ferocity and
speed that the industry

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:37.860
evolved so quickly.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.950
The number of wallets
that were purchasing NFTs

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:46.550
skyrocketed the number
of transactions of NFTs,

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.830
across the board skyrocketed.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:53.610
And the prices for many
NFT artists also went up,

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:56.760
but that came down very, very fast.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.560
Once red hot NFTs are
now cooling off a bit.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.360
The secondary market for the majority

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.360
of Beeple's paintings today is down 90-95%

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:12.293
from the peak, as is most NFT art.

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:16.800
But even more recently,

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.340
NFTs had been on the rise once again.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:21.640
And even with significant value volatility

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:26.000
in NFT artwork, NFT
collectibles like NBA top shot

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:29.020
and NFT-based video games
like "Axie infinity"

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:31.030
have surged in popularity.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:32.180
The correction that kinda happened

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.520
with the NFT art side is healthy.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.070
And what we're gonna
see come out of that now

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.600
are projects that actually have utility,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.300
that will be able to affect a
much wider amount of people.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.260
I think that's where the excitement is.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:44.610
And many companies are betting

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:48.830
that NFTs will enter the
video game world in a big way,

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.380
which if successful, could open NFTs

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.050
to a massive new audience,

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:56.480
and forever change the way
we value digital objects.

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.020
We are in the process of an exodus

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:03.120
from the physical world
to these digital worlds

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.490
that have been created
over the last 20 years.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:06.900
And that continue to be created

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.560
and where people are
spending an increasing amount

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:10.963
of their conscious time.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.630
You may have heard by now that NFTs

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:27.653
stands for non-fungible token.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.480
But explaining exactly what
that means can get complicated.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:33.820
So for our purposes,

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.010
what you need to know is that
through blockchain technology,

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:40.620
digital objects, like a JPEG
file or a virtual sneaker,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:42.810
or even a meme that you'd
normally think about

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:47.160
as being infinitely reproducible
can now be made scarce.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:49.480
So for example, if you're resourceful,

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.220
the video you're watching right now

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:52.950
could be downloaded from YouTube,

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.740
and you could copy and paste
it as much as you'd like.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:59.079
In theory, it is infinitely reproducible.

00:02:59.079 --> 00:03:01.890
Tokenizing or minting is the
process you can go through

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:05.960
via blockchain, where you can
create a record of scarcity.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:06.793
In the blockchain,

00:03:06.793 --> 00:03:07.970
you can enter into a ledger

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.580
that there are only 1,000
versions of this video

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:13.440
or 100, or even just one.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.890
Taking something that was
infinitely replicatable

00:03:15.890 --> 00:03:18.270
and giving it artificial scarcity.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.510
And if you're rushing out to try

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:21.040
and download this video now,

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.610
thinking you'll make a quick buck,

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.040
that's not exactly how it works.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.730
Only the holder of the NFT

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.530
and what are known as digital wallets

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.360
can actually own the item.

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:31.870
And they can choose to resell them

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.290
in exchange for cryptocurrencies.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.930
But while we have seen
some high sale prices,

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:40.590
are buying these NFTs
actually a good investment?

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:43.054
I would hesitate to call
NFTs an asset class,

00:03:43.054 --> 00:03:44.470
they are an asset.

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:46.600
In the same way that I
would hesitate to call art

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:48.530
an asset class, art is an asset.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:51.070
But for it to achieve an asset class,

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:52.280
it really needs to be something

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:56.200
that you can buy and
sell, and buy and sell.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.040
This is Bloomberg's art
reporter, James Tarmy.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.600
So all you have to do when you
think about the art market,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:04.077
and you think about the
prices that are paid for these

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:06.727
$10 million paintings,
$20 million paintings,

00:04:06.727 --> 00:04:08.260
$30 million paintings,

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:10.090
is thinking about what kind of liquidity

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:12.380
you need to have to spend $30 million

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.243
on something that you
cannot resell anytime soon.

00:04:16.243 --> 00:04:18.340
Sold!

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:19.850
There is not a line of people

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:21.710
waiting to spend $30
million on the painting

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:22.543
that you just bought.

00:04:22.543 --> 00:04:23.610
There might be one other person

00:04:23.610 --> 00:04:25.050
or maybe two other people,

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.290
but that's no guarantee
that those two other people

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:30.410
wanna buy that painting when
you wanna sell that painting.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:32.910
And this is similar in the NFT space.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:36.870
The number of people able to spend

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:39.740
large sums of cryptocurrency on NFTs

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.460
is relatively small.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.510
And a lot of people became very interested

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:46.120
in jumping on the bandwagon.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:48.930
There was a world that was opened up

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:53.230
to a general public that
they didn't know about.

00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:58.210
And that seems like a world
of infinite possibilities.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:03.210
And so corporations suddenly
started releasing NFTs.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:06.940
Charmin toilet paper released an NFT.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:11.350
I just got an email about
Campbell's soups first NFT.

00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:12.910
And this isn't just about artworks.

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.970
This is also about the various NFTs

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:18.340
that are connected to
games and virtual worlds,

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:19.260
and so forth.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:21.990
People are connecting houses to NFTs,

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:25.240
they're connecting
physical artworks to NFTs,

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:30.170
they're connecting portions
of an entire soccer team

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:32.980
in a Mexican soccer league to NFTs.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:36.420
And then something weird
started to happen, right?

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:39.800
More people were making
NFTs than buying NFTs.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:44.800
So you had a lot of people
trying to jump on a bandwagon

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:48.893
that might not really have
existed in the first place.

00:05:49.730 --> 00:05:51.450
And while the initial boom of interest

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.450
did drive more people to participate

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:55.763
in the NFT marketplace than before,

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:59.930
those numbers are still relatively small.

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:03.320
According to a 2021
report by nonfungible.com,

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:04.630
weekly active wallets,

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:07.780
in theory, the number of
people who bought and sold NFTs

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:11.580
in a given week peaked at only 40,000.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:14.090
And if NFTs are going to
become the asset class

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:15.440
of the digital future,

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:16.870
the number of buyers and sellers

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.760
in the market must increase by a lot.

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.570
There's still a tremendous
amount of friction.

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:23.940
To get a Metamask wallet,

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:27.280
to understand the idea
of holding private keys,

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:28.963
to engage with this world,

00:06:30.297 --> 00:06:31.910
it's a process.

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:33.530
This is Sam Englebardt.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:35.510
He's the general partner
at a venture business

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:37.530
called Galaxy Interactive.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:40.120
They currently manage a $500 million fund

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.020
investing in technology and content

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:44.710
aimed towards the future
of digital worlds.

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:48.170
I spend a lot of time these days,

00:06:48.170 --> 00:06:52.650
first, trying to just define
what the metaverse is.

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.993
And then make sure we're all
talking about the same thing,

00:06:54.993 --> 00:06:57.470
because I think we're
still quite a ways away

00:06:57.470 --> 00:07:00.760
from an immersive world
like "Ready Player One,"

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.400
being something that we all inhabit.

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:04.660
Except for eating, sleeping,

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:05.750
and bathroom breaks,

00:07:05.750 --> 00:07:08.490
whatever people wanna do,
they do it in the Oasis.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:11.230
I think we are very, very close,

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:13.920
and witnessing every day though,

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:16.950
the exodus from a purely physical world

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:20.810
to an increasingly immersive
synthetic digital world

00:07:20.810 --> 00:07:23.660
that doesn't look like
one place we all go to,

00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:26.730
but it's actually just the amalgamation

00:07:26.730 --> 00:07:30.540
of the different digital worlds

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:33.333
that we experience on our various devices.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.010
And a lot of players

00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:41.980
are investing in this
future digital world.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:44.020
A recent Bloomberg intelligence report

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:46.220
believes that the market
opportunity for the metaverse

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:49.970
could be $800 billion by 2024.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:51.460
And for Galaxy Interactive,

00:07:51.460 --> 00:07:54.380
one of their biggest
investments has been in gaming.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:57.940
I don't believe long-term

00:07:57.940 --> 00:08:01.540
that just by making something an NFT,

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:03.300
it's gonna be interesting enough

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.970
to be all that meaningful unless you build

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:07.703
some sort of a game or metagame

00:08:07.703 --> 00:08:09.883
into the process of collecting itself.

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:18.060
Merging NFTs with games

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:20.390
has already seen some great success.

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:24.620
One of the earliest breakout
NFTs was "CryptoKitties,"

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:29.070
cartoon cats that users can
buy, sell, and even breed.

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:31.340
The NFT became so popular at one point

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:35.250
that it clogged the entire
Ethereum network back in 2017.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:38.470
And it continues to have
an active community today.

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:40.770
And one of the drivers of
increased digital wallets

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.430
post the NFT boom in early 2021

00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:46.520
has been a game called "Axie Infinity."

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:49.990
These pokemon-like creatures
are all unique NFTs

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:51.970
that a user can buy into and level up

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.050
by playing in a new business model

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:55.680
known as play-to-earn.

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.270
What could be better than a free game?

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:01.530
Well, how about a game
that pays you to play it.

00:09:01.530 --> 00:09:02.920
So far, it's allowed people

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:05.850
in developing economies like
the Philippines and Vietnam

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.973
to earn real money through playing.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.770
And now a team of veteran
video game developers

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:18.400
at Mythical Games are working

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:22.500
on their first NFT based
game, "Blankos Block Party."

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:25.570
With real toys in real life,
the way to keep their value

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:28.260
is you put them in a box,
you put them on a shelf,

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:29.460
you leave them there,

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:32.210
but with "Blankos," as you play with them,

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:33.380
they get better.

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:36.420
Most games, if you bought
Spider-Man character

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.220
or you bought a "League of Legends" skin,

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:39.260
they're virtual items.

00:09:39.260 --> 00:09:41.130
They stay within that ecosystem.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:42.880
Once you buy them, you bought them, right?

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:44.260
There's nothing else you
could really do with them

00:09:44.260 --> 00:09:45.160
at that point.

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:45.993
And what we're doing

00:09:45.993 --> 00:09:47.740
in a kind of experimentation with Blankos

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:49.330
is players own them.

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:51.590
They can sell these assets,
they can level them up,

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:53.870
and mash them up to create
new ones and all that.

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:56.088
So it really gives the
players a lot of freedom

00:09:56.088 --> 00:09:58.560
to build, play, and earn.

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.330
This is Mythical Games CEO, John Linden.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:04.800
He sees games as a great
means to introduce more people

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:07.130
into the NFT and crypto world.

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:09.600
So one of the goals in
Mythical was we really wanted

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:12.970
to bring this concept of
blockchain and NFT assets

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:14.640
to the mainstream gamer, right?

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:17.580
So maybe someone that doesn't
fully understand crypto

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.410
and is not an investor in Ethereum,

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:20.720
we really wanted to make sure

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.720
that they could partake in this economy.

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:26.850
And his success in this space

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:29.570
isn't necessarily about
having big headline grabbing

00:10:29.570 --> 00:10:31.020
single asset sales,

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:34.480
but rather having a much
larger volume of sales overall.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.650
We're not thinking about
it in terms of a $10 item

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:38.520
is worth $1 million the next day.

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:41.130
We're thinking about
it terms of a $10 item

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:42.520
that becomes worth $25.

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:44.570
And a $25 item that becomes worth $40.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.950
And a $40 item that becomes worth $75.

00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:47.783
And those are still,

00:10:47.783 --> 00:10:48.760
if you think about it, those are amazing.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.900
They don't sound sexy, but they're amazing

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:53.650
returns on investments, right?

00:10:53.650 --> 00:10:55.010
So we're definitely thinking about it

00:10:55.010 --> 00:10:57.170
in terms of something that's attainable.

00:10:57.170 --> 00:10:59.140
Something that the average consumer

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:00.470
can actually partake in.

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:03.630
Latest trades, $55, $20.

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:05.710
Not kind of a new fine art

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:08.300
to were only a very few select people

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:09.690
can actually see that appreciation

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:11.620
because the numbers are just so ridiculous

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:13.940
that you can't can't participate.

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:15.610
But growing the market for NFTs

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:18.700
currently can come at
some pretty large costs.

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:20.990
Each transaction requires a ton of energy

00:11:20.990 --> 00:11:22.820
from computers all around the world,

00:11:22.820 --> 00:11:25.840
potentially resulting in
increased carbon emissions.

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:28.040
And although the Ethereum
network is hard at work

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.280
to reduce this overall energy consumption,

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:32.370
it does remain to this day, too high

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.970
to support a scaled version of this model.

00:11:34.970 --> 00:11:37.480
What we're starting to
see technologically now

00:11:37.480 --> 00:11:42.480
is the need to advance the underlying tech

00:11:42.760 --> 00:11:45.150
so that you can have a business

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:49.250
around the purchase, and
sale, and trading of objects

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:52.273
that are $1, $5, $20, $50.

00:11:55.820 --> 00:11:57.750
That's partly why Mythical Games

00:11:57.750 --> 00:12:00.000
is using their own blockchain technology

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.840
within their Mythical economic engine.

00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:04.290
But it's also because they believe

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:06.890
that creating and selling
an NFT game engine

00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:10.040
will help create more
NFT games in the future.

00:12:10.040 --> 00:12:11.480
What we set out to do
at the very beginning

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:13.010
was to build this economic engine

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:14.720
to where we could ultimately have

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:16.720
a lot of the top games in the world

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:19.040
be able to access this type of technology,

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:20.950
have the regulatory side covered,

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:22.520
have access to marketplace tech.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:23.870
Things that normal game studios

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:25.720
wouldn't be building themselves.

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:28.030
We think that this type of economy

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:30.180
will really permeate
through every game type.

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:33.240
And honestly, I can see
almost every game out there

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:35.430
could have some version of this.

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:36.640
But building the technology

00:12:36.640 --> 00:12:39.650
is only the first step to building value.

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:42.010
Gaming also offers another important step

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:44.430
in this process, community.

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:46.460
Blockchain doesn't make a game better.

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:48.089
We kinda joke here that if your game sucks

00:12:48.089 --> 00:12:51.220
without blockchain, it's gonna
suck with blockchain, right?

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:54.020
What makes this technology
powerful and interesting

00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:57.030
is the ability to ignite a community.

00:12:57.030 --> 00:12:59.280
And if you build the right community,

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:02.320
then the things you own,

00:13:02.320 --> 00:13:04.680
digital objects in a lot of cases,

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.830
will be valuable within that community

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:09.490
and people are gonna wanna own them,

00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:11.450
and they're gonna wanna trade
them amongst each other.

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:13.900
We seen an amazing community
formed around Blankos.

00:13:13.900 --> 00:13:15.790
And even before the game came out,

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:18.040
we've had about $1 million of Blankos sold

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:19.810
because players saw that value,

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:21.390
and they collaborated with each other.

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:24.040
And were excited by the
outcome of where that can go.

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.700
For someone outside of
the Blankos community,

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:32.680
It might seem outrageous to spend $5,000

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:34.670
on a virtual doll.

00:13:34.670 --> 00:13:35.503
Just like for many,

00:13:35.503 --> 00:13:38.230
it's outrageous to spend $69 million

00:13:38.230 --> 00:13:40.190
on this Beeple's piece.

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:45.110
Or for spending $120,000 on a
banana duct taped to a wall.

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:49.540
Value for things that have no use value

00:13:49.540 --> 00:13:51.563
is entirely the result of perception.

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:55.810
A Picasso is just oil paint on canvas.

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:58.340
It's worth nothing, it does nothing.

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:00.980
It is valuable because we as a society

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:02.763
have decided that it's valuable.

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:08.730
Could NFTs become a brief,
weird piece of history?

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:11.880
Absolutely.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:14.520
Could they become an integral part

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:18.530
of the way that we engage with each other

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:20.100
and companies online?

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:20.933
Absolutely.

